		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a143" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts284" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts285">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Well does this have to do with reasoning in general In other words could you generalize about his method of reasoning that it would be a method that anybody could use for reasoning?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a144" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts286" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts287">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Well except that there are people who have great insights through ways of reasoning that don't get caught I mean that was the big thing with Prazel that here was the totally opposite what looked like totally opposite messy slapdash imprecise parenthetical parenthetical and yet knowing what about having a sense of what's really important and what the main things are that need to be done need to be attended to and relying on insight more than on systematic careful development Systematic careful development or systematic careful proof In other words Both But did Kreisel have actual proofs that went with his insights Well I mean that's part of what the whole issue is with now with the reexamination of his work and whether what really had he done in some cases you could say well he laid out the main ideas and you just had to fill in the details In other cases it might be an enormous struggle to fill in the details but it can still be done.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a145" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts288" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts289">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>In other cases you don't even know what the details are that are supposed to be filled in.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a146" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts290" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts291">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>You don't even know what he claims to have done.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a147" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts292" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts293">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah so it's a bag mixed bag of things But there are assertions he made about what is so.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a148" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts294" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts295">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah but some of those assertions are rather vague and he worked in collaboration with a lot of people so that they would take over the job of doing a more standard kind of proof with details but say the leading ideas would be his or partly his.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a149" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts296" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts297">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>So he would say this should be so because such and such and such this ought to be the case and then somebody would show come along and show how it was the case What's the question off the top of this talking off the top of your head or just a different way of thinking convincing yourself of things But isn't mathematics supposed to be convincing other people too?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a150" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts298" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts299">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah But he did he was able to convince other people of defendant could he convince Tarski that anything he said was so?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a151" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts300" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts301">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Not necessarily I doubt it.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a152" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts302" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts303">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>So Tarski In other words if he were a student in Tarski seminar then I think they're Yeah a hopeless confrontational Yeah I mean I actually never saw them together.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a153" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts304" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts305">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>The year he came to Berkeley Tarsky was on leave but the people in Berkeley didn't know what to make of what he was saying Hankin and others who were targeted trained They knew there was something there but they sort of they had to figure it out for themselves in a way that would be acceptable to the but what they figured out wasn't the same as what he was saying, it was somehow related But Tarski didn't describe him as a charlatan or did he I don't know actually what he had to say about Tarski.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
