				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yes Every last detail everything is accounted for Everything's accounted for But it sounds kind of tedious in a way then.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a160" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts318" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts319">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah it can be.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a161" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts320" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts321">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I mean that's Alonso Church was that way The worst yeah but he never got off the ground So how did he keep it from being tedious?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a162" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts322" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts323">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>By talking about things that were important really interesting So the marvel is that he was able to do that he was able to see to it that all the details were attended to without having that be the just a matter of fussing with details without being lost in them with everything So if it's like a good mystery story there are all these details around that add up the sum of the parts makes the whole and that every detail is a telling you don't put a detail in there without having it or any story any detail that appears in a story has some connection with the story and eventually you see why that detail is there to tie up the whole story and that there's nothing there in a good story, there's no detail that's there for no reason everything is there for some reason and in the It's not only that everything's there for a reason but that everything that needs to be there is there Everything that needs to be there but there's not one thing too much and not one thing too little and it all needs to be there in order to make the whole thing hold together but nothing is there for extraneous reasons and everything that's there has a reason and you couldn't have get to the end without all those details But is that true?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a163" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts324" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts325">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>I mean it depends I mean it's like the design for a house Well that true of him I would say it's like plans for a house I mean somebody like Bernard could use plans that didn't have to be so detailed as say what Thad provided and maybe Thad put in details that didn't have to be there or didn't put in things that did need to be there but what a consumer or somebody who's trying to follow this needs is going to vary from person to person.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a164" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts326" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts327">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Some people would say well I need more details More details I'm thinking more in terms of the end product.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a165" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts328" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts329">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Let's take this house and all the details that are in it.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
				</ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
			</ANNOTATION>
		<ANNOTATION>
			<ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a166" TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts330" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts331">
				<ANNOTATION_VALUE>Well there's nothing in this house that there are things in this house let's say that don't absolutely have to be here.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
